course: dictionary
lesson: 7367
title: Pleasure: happy feelings and polite replies
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Pleasure is a noun. It means the feeling of being happy or enjoying something. When you read a sentence, look at the words around pleasure to find its meaning. «Children who read for pleasure» is a clue: they read because they enjoy it, not because they have to.
explain: Pleasure has some useful phrases. «My pleasure» is a polite answer when someone says thank you. It means "I was glad to help." «It was a pleasure to meet you» means meeting you made the speaker happy.
explain: «For pleasure» means for fun, not for work or school. «With pleasure» means gladly, as in «Will you help me? With pleasure!» To check the meaning, swap in "gladly" or "for fun" and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Pleasure means the feeling of being happy or enjoying something.
fact: A pleasure is something that makes you happy.
fact: «My pleasure» is a polite reply when someone thanks you.
fact: «For pleasure» means for fun, not for work.
fact: «With pleasure» means gladly, yes I will; it is a more formal way of saying yes.
exercise: «My pleasure», said Mum when Sam thanked her for the sandwich. What does it mean? | *I was glad to help | I want my money back | The sandwich was really yours | Please go away
exercise: Ava reads books in bed «for pleasure», not because her teacher asked. What does for pleasure mean? | For a test at school | *For fun | Very slowly | Because she has to
exercise: Someone asks, «Will you help me carry the bags?» You answer, «With pleasure». What do you mean? | *Yes, I will gladly | No, I am too tired | I will think about it later | Only if you pay me
test: «It was a pleasure to meet you.» What does this sentence tell us? | *Meeting you made the speaker happy | Meeting you was hard work | The meeting was cancelled | The speaker did not want to meet you
test: Which phrase is a polite reply when someone thanks you? | Pleasure to | For pleasure | Take pleasure in | *My pleasure
exam: In «Studies have found that children who read for pleasure tend to know more words», what does for pleasure mean? | Only at school | In a hurry, to finish quickly | Because they are told to | *Because they enjoy it, for fun
